PROPOSAL (not applied) — auto-resync the host seal counter from the
device's reported floor before sealing. For John's approval.
Tested: applies cleanly to seal_client.py; new logic unit-tested
(resync-up, throttle, lower-floor no-op, anti-oracle refusal, missing
log no-op) against a temp HOME — the real ~/.ssh/watcher_seal_ctr
was not touched.

Problem (verified 2026-10-05):
  The S3 re-verifies every sealed envelope itself (seal_dev_verify=true)
  and ratchets its accept floor on every boot:
      floor = last_persisted_high_water + 32        (seal_dev.c:966, D4)
  Every S3 reboot/flash during bench work can therefore push the floor up
  to 32 past the host's persisted counter (~/.ssh/watcher_seal_ctr).
  The host has a documented manual recovery (`seal_client.py --resync
  <floor>`) but nothing runs it, so each incident recovered only by
  "eating loud REPLAY drops walking past it" (seal_client.py docstring):
  every rejected attempt still increments the host counter by 1.
  John reported this three times in one session (06:35, 09:01, 09:43 UTC);
  telemetry.log shows e.g. "SEAL: REPLAY ctr 24196 <= 24225 from muse-01
  - DROPPED" (2026-10-05 09:51:38).

Fix: a pre-send guard in seal_client.seal_command() that reads the most
recent seal_floor from the bridge telemetry log (the device reports it in
every heartbeat; link.c:581) and resyncs when the floor has run past our
counter. Safety properties:
  - resync_counter() only ever RAISES the counter (max(own, floor));
    monotonicity is preserved, so this can never cause a REPLAY itself.
  - The existing anti-oracle cap (refuse floor > own + 2^20) is kept: a
    corrupt/stale log line can at worst no-op, never burn counter space.
  - A stale or missing telemetry.log yields no floor -> no-op.
  - Check is throttled to once per 60s so per-chunk flash sealing
    (474 sealed commands per transfer) doesn't re-scan the log each time.
  - Known limitation: the heartbeat reports the BOOT floor (seal_dev_floor
    is set once at boot, seal_dev.c:966); the live reject threshold is the
    runtime high-water, which can be higher. Resyncing to the boot floor
    closes the common post-reboot gap; any residual gap still self-heals
    by walking, as today.

Apply from host-agent/mqtt-bridge/ with:  git apply seal_autoresync_PROPOSAL.diff
(The diff below is also saved separately as seal_autoresync_PROPOSAL.diff.)

